    <!-- Watch Out For -->
    <section class="exam-section" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #fff3cd 0%, #ffe5a0 100%); border-left-color: #ffc107;">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation section-icon" style="color: #856404;"></i> Common Misconceptions</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Infinite Loops vs. Recursion:</strong> Students often think an infinite recursion just "loops." It must be clarified that it causes a StackOverflowError due to memory exhaustion.</li>
            <li><strong>Unwinding Phase:</strong> Many believe the function "ends" at the base case; they must understand that the stack must then pop every frame to return the final value.</li>
            <li><strong>Variable Persistence:</strong> Believing local variables are shared across calls. Each stack frame has its own unique instance of local data.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Big Ideas -->
    <section class="content-section" style="border-top-color: var(--accent-color);">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb section-icon"></i> Big Ideas to Consider</h2>
        <div class="learning-points">
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Infinite Regress:</strong> If a solution always requires a smaller version of itself, does the logic ever have a true foundation?</li>
                <li><strong>Mathematical Elegance:</strong> Why do we value "elegant" recursive solutions in CS when they are often less efficient in terms of physical hardware resources?</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

